{"birds": [{"name": "House Finch", "sci": "Haemorhous mexicanus", "n": 31, "first": "9:45 AM", "last": "10:40 AM", "ts": 1794667549, "mins": [585, 586, 586, 586, 605, 609, 612, 612, 613, 613, 614, 615, 616, 620, 621, 622, 622, 623, 623, 624, 625, 633, 633, 634, 634, 634, 635, 636, 637, 639, 640], "anim": null, "trunk": false, "fact": "Native to the West, house finches were released on Long Island in 1940 as pet-shop “Hollywood finches” and spread across the East. Redder males are favored by females.", "vox": "sounds/voice/haemorhous-mexicanus.mp3?v=1793113005", "snd": "sounds/haemorhous-mexicanus.mp3?v=1793113108", "sndby": "Ted Floyd, xeno-canto XC622745, CC BY-NC-SA 4.0", "key": "housefinch", "cm": 14.0, "img": "cutouts/housefinch.png?v=1793046043", "w": 800, "h": 581, "len": 662.0, "v": 1793046043, "beak": [0.12, 0.13, "L"], "mask": {"c": 32, "r": 23, "b": "B+AAAAfwAQoP8AHOH/gB7wf8Af8D/gH/B/8A3wf/AP4H/4zgB//fwAP/zwAD//4AAf/+AAD//AAAf/gAAD/8AA/+/AAH4B4AB+APAB6AD4A4AAeA8AAAAMAAAAA="}}, {"name": "American Goldfinch", "sci": "Spinus tristis", "n": 8, "first": "9:47 AM", "last": "10:21 AM", "ts": 1794667677, "mins": [587, 613, 613, 619, 620, 620, 621, 621], "anim": null, "trunk": false, "fact": "One of the strictest vegetarians among birds. Goldfinches nest late in summer, when thistle seeds are ripe, and swap bright yellow for olive-brown in winter.", "vox": "sounds/voice/spinus-tristis.mp3?v=1793113053", "snd": "sounds/spinus-tristis.mp3?v=1793113090", "sndby": "Will Sweet, xeno-canto XC453934, CC BY-NC-SA 4.0", "key": "goldfinch", "cm": 12.0, "img": "cutouts/goldfinch.png?v=1793990895", "w": 451, "h": 369, "len": 415.0, "v": 1793990895, "beak": [0.206, 0.15, "L"], "mask": {"c": 32, "r": 26, "b": "ADwAAAD+AAAB/wAAA/8AAAP/gAAA/8AAAH/AAAD/8AAA//AAAP/4DwD/+B4Af/w4AH/+8AA//8AAP/8AAB//AAAH/4AAB//AAA/PwAAeQeAA/gHwAfgA+APIAHwPAAAgHAAAADgAAAA="}}, {"name": "Tufted Titmouse", "sci": "Baeolophus bicolor", "n": 1, "first": "9:48 AM", "last": "9:48 AM", "ts": 1794667701, "mins": [588], "anim": null, "trunk": false, "fact": "Titmice line their nests with hair, and they have been seen plucking it from live raccoons, dogs, and even people.", "vox": "sounds/voice/baeolophus-bicolor.mp3?v=1793112971", "snd": "sounds/baeolophus-bicolor.mp3?v=1793113083", "sndby": "Jim Berry, xeno-canto XC649331, CC BY-NC-SA 4.0", "key": "titmouse", "cm": 16.5, "img": "cutouts/titmouse.png?v=1793046043", "w": 574, "h": 800, "len": 653.0, "v": 1793046043, "beak": [0.2, 0.25, "L"], "mask": {"c": 32, "r": 45, "b": "cAAAAHAAAAAwAAAAIAAAAGgDAAB8B4AAPB8AAD5/AAA+/4AAeP+AAHv/wAB7/8AA+P/gAPhn4ACbH/AADz/wAA9/+AAPf/wABb/8AB+//gAO//4ABt//AAfP/wAHx/+AB+P/gAPh/8AHiP/AB47/wAeH/+AHofvgBvOB8AD3APgAfAB4APgAPAD4AB4A+AAfAPwADwA3AA8APwAHAD4AAAAfAAAAGwAAABgAAAAMAAAADAAA"}}, {"name": "Downy Woodpecker", "sci": "Dryobates pubescens", "n": 1, "first": "9:54 AM", "last": "9:54 AM", "ts": 1794668068, "mins": [594], "anim": null, "trunk": false, "fact": "North America’s smallest woodpecker. It is small enough to forage on thin weed stalks and twigs that bigger woodpeckers can’t cling to.", "vox": "sounds/voice/dryobates-pubescens.mp3?v=1793112995", "snd": "sounds/dryobates-pubescens.mp3?v=1793113092", "sndby": "Thomas G. Graves, xeno-canto XC89855, CC BY-NC-ND 2.5", "key": "downy", "cm": 16.0, "img": "cutouts/downy.png?v=1793045350", "w": 486, "h": 800, "len": 707.0, "v": 1793045350, "beak": [0.59, 0.1, "L"], "mask": {"c": 32, "r": 53, "b": "AAAAAP/4AAD//AAA//wAAP/8IAD//DwA//wf4H/8D/x//Af+f/wH/3/8A/9//AP/f/wD/3/8B/9//A//f/wf/n/8P/x//H/8f/7//H/+//x////8f////D////x////8f////H////h////4f///+D////A////wP///4D///+A////gP///wD///8A///+AP///gD///wA///4AP//+AD///AA///wAP//sAD//4AA//8AAP//AAD//wAA//4AAP/+AAD//gAA//4AAP/8AAD//AAA="}}, {"name": "Dark-eyed Junco", "sci": "Junco hyemalis", "n": 3, "first": "10:05 AM", "last": "10:19 AM", "ts": 1794668744, "mins": [605, 619, 619], "anim": null, "trunk": false, "fact": "Often called “snowbirds” because they show up as winter arrives. The white outer tail